---
term: Natural resistance
aka:
  - Right of resistance
section: plurality-and-actuality
order: 10
definition: The capacity of any party that rejects an end to resist, oppose, appeal, and set up an alternative course of action.
seeAlso:
  - material-actuality
  - immanent-authority
  - recourse
  - limit-of-arbitration
---
Human agency allows for different and incompatible purposes. So no individual or collective has an exclusive right to demand that everyone accept its purpose. Resistance is itself an exercise of agency.

The existence of resistance doesn't establish that it's correct, just as the existence of an established authority doesn't establish that its continued exercise is legitimate. Neither agreement nor disagreement gives a universal moral verdict. The absence of consensus doesn't remove the right to act, and the assertion of an absolute purpose doesn't remove the right of others to resist it.

Each party can pursue its own ends by its own foundational principles, and their actions and competing claims meet material reality. The thesis doesn't presume that incompatible purposes must reconcile, that competing principles must become commensurable, or that disagreement must produce a conclusion that everyone accepts.

Isaiah Berlin calls the view that human ends are many, and sometimes incompatible, *value pluralism* ("Two Concepts of Liberty," 1958). Political philosophy calls the response to an authority that a party rejects the *right of resistance*, or the right of revolution. Locke grounds it in the people's power to remove a government that breaks their trust (*Second Treatise of Government*, 1689, chapter 19). The thesis speaks of resistance both as a natural capacity and as a right.
